# Result JSONs ARE tracked. They used to be ignored on the grounds that "raw JSON is
# uploaded to S3 anyway" — that was never true in a way that survives: the results
# bucket is created by the shared stack and DESTROYED at teardown, so the S3 copy dies
# with the run and the local file was the only copy. Consequences, both hit for real:
#
#   - `runner summary` rebuilds SUMMARY.md's table from whatever result files exist
#     locally, so a scenario whose JSON is gone silently loses its row. The mysql and
#     snowflake JSONs were purged from a worktree by the /tmp cleaner and their four
#     published rows disappeared on the next refresh.
#   - the sizing page cites a run file per entry for provenance; with the files absent,
#     nobody can audit a quoted number back to its run.
#
# They are ~100-350 KB each. Trim old runs deliberately (keep the latest per scenario,
# plus anything a doc or the sizing page cites) rather than by letting them be ignored.

# Terraform local state — never commit
.terraform/
.terraform.lock.hcl
terraform.tfstate
terraform.tfstate.*

# Built binaries staged for upload
runner/dist/
seeders/dist/

# Stray local builds (e.g. accidental `go build` without -o)
runner/runner
cleanup-lambda/bootstrap
cleanup-lambda/bootstrap.zip
cleanup-lambda/cleanup-lambda
